Bartlett to the rescue.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.420
MARK: That one work?

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:41.920
Oh, yeah.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:49.110
JEFF MAGEE: All right, so
thank you, Mark, for that.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.880
Thank you, Ila for
the invitation.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.780
I'm excited about ICoN.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.120
Attacking this problem at
multiple levels is really,

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:01.320
I think, an excellent way
to make some progress.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.670
And so I would encourage
you to pursue that as

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.680
much as possible.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.870
We try to work at
three different levels,

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:11.740
and I find it
really overwhelming.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.750
So I think you had
six or seven there.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.160
And so good luck with that.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:23.730
But-- [LAUGHING]
Pointer, no pointer, OK.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:29.040
All right, so I
spent a long time

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.590
working on dendrites
when Mark was in the lab.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.980
That's pretty much all we did.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.890
More recently, we're studying
it-- thank you, again,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.960
Bartlett.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:46.515
We're studying a different
function of dendrites, maybe.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.920
Just for the pointer?

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:56.230
And OK, that's a problem.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:03:04.000
But-- just for the
pointer, you think?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.070
CREW: Oh, for the pointer.

00:03:06.070 --> 00:03:07.970
JEFF MAGEE: Yeah.

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.170
Perfect, all right.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.790
CREW: This works fine.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.650
JEFF MAGEE: Two of them.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.750
So yes, we're trying to increase
our understanding of how

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.460
brains learn, OK?

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.280
Again, no small task.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.700
And we're sort of inching
our way along, hopefully,

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.260
systematically.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.840
It's been thought
for a long time

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.240
that learning produces adaptive
changes in certain brain

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:36.290
regions.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:41.630
And of course, those changes are
adaptive because they improve

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.820
the behavior of the animal.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:49.880
And so it's also been thought--

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.670
or known for a long
time that those

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:56.270
changes in particular
brain regions

00:03:56.270 --> 00:04:00.260
are produced by
alterations in the strength

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.350
of synaptic connections between
neurons in those regions.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.470
And this synaptic
plasticity alters

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:10.850
the input-output
transformation there in a way

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.930
that improves the behavior.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:16.160
So now I'm going to
just bring up two issues

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:18.560
that you could say
complicate this.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.410
And the first is just
pure numbers, OK?

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:24.800
In the little mouse
hippocampal subregion

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.040
I'm going to talk about,
there are 6 billion synapses,

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.850
and of course, really
astronomical numbers

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.770
in our brains.

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:37.130
And so it brings
up the point, how

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:41.210
is it possible for these
synaptic plasticity mechanisms

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.250
to find the right
synapses and change them

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:48.150
appropriately all within a short
amount of time as possible?

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.180
And so this is one problem.

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:55.580
And the other one is probably
an even bigger issue, something

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.140
that you're all familiar with.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.480
And that's what I've
labeled synapse depth.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:05.150
And we'll just use this little
multilayer three-layer network

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:06.590
as an analogy here.

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.790
It's not a very good one,
but it'll serve the purpose.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.800
Here's the input
layer at the bottom.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.290
You think of sensory
input coming in here.

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:19.730
And then these
cells make synapses

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:21.312
on to the middle
or hidden layer.

00:05:21.312 --> 00:05:23.270
And then the middle hidden
layer makes synapses

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:27.980
on the output layer, the output
of which drives some action.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.620
So of course,
multilayer networks

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.090
are quite powerful
because they can

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:36.590
produce complex input-output
transformations.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:40.940
The trick is, of course, how to
change these 6 billion synapses

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:44.390
in light of the fact that there
are 60 billion other synapses

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:48.290
and parameters downstream
of them, between them

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:52.610
and the output region,
whose output you're

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:55.610
trying to optimize by
changing the weights of all

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:57.380
of these synapses.

00:05:57.380 --> 00:06:00.920
Of course, Hebbian plasticities
these aren't up to this task

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:05.010
because they're blind to all
of these downstream parameters,

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:05.510
OK?

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:10.370
What's needed is some
kind of additional signal

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:16.160
that brings back information
about these parameters

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:22.490
and helps assist this synaptic
plasticity process right here.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.150
This is, of course, backprop
in artificial neural networks.

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:30.770
And suffice it to say that
it is completely unknown how

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:36.011
this works or what this
signal is in real brains.

00:06:36.011 --> 00:06:38.810
OK, so that's what I'm
going to talk about,

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:43.760
is a little adaptive change in
hippocampal population activity

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.600
that occurs during a very simple
behavior, a simple learning

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.700
behavior in mice.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.880
And then I'll point out
the synaptic plasticity

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.210
that's responsible for
this adaptive change.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.920
And then we'll go
into some detail

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.360
about the properties of the
instructive signal that is

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:04.220
directing the plasticity, OK?

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:06.680
So here's the behavior.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.060
We work in mice.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.640
They're head-fixed mice.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:13.310
Here's one, an old
one, back in the day

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.370
when Mark was with
us, from Seb Royer.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.850
And you can see that this
mouse is on a treadmill.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:21.680
The treadmill has got
some features on it.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.850
And they're trained to run
a lap, about 2 meters, 180

00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:29.270
centimeters, and stop and
lick their water reward.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.730
Very, very, very simple,
but that's the learning.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:35.060
That's what they learn, where
to run to, and stop, and lick.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:39.740
And I'll show you some
behavioral data in a minute.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.260
During this, we're going
to make one of either two

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:43.850
kinds of recordings,
the first I'll

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:46.310
show you, some
population recordings

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:52.340
from a set of about 300, 400
neurons pyramidal cells in CA1.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.790
And you can see the
world famous place cells.

00:07:55.790 --> 00:07:58.310
From six of these
shown here, you

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.400
can see that they
tile the environment.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:03.870
Essentially, these
are calcium signals,

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.960
of course, that rise and
fall because the action

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.410
potential firing rate of
these cells rises and falls.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:16.140
Here's one of these place
fields, just now in location.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:21.120
And you can see the heat maps
of the place field, the firing

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.200
rate, if you will, for all
of these six neurons here.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.710
And they've been sorted so
that they tile the environment.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:32.110
You're going to see a lot of
these in the next 30 minutes.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:33.360
So that's what they look like.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:36.426
The activity has
been peak scaled.

00:08:36.426 --> 00:08:38.460
OK, so here's the behavior.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:39.870
It's very simple.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.975
Christine is going to
have several days of sort

00:08:42.975 --> 00:08:45.300
of pre-training,
where the animal's

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.310
on a belt that is blank.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.340
It has none of these
somatosensory cues on it.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.080
And the reward position on the
belt changes from lap to lap.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:58.440
And you can see,
here's the licking.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.300
Each one of these
little ticks is a lick.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.700
And the animal's just
licking all over the place,

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.320
trying to find the reward.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.150
And they're running at rather
a uniform, a constant yet slow

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:12.580
velocity across the track.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:19.210
And here are a population
of 400 or so CA1 place

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.700
cells that are
recorded simultaneously

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:22.670
with this behavior.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:24.580
And you can see,
again, they've been

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.430
sorted by the position
of their place field.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:31.790
And there's a uniform tiling
of the entire environment.

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:35.140
Now, next day is learning day.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:38.170
She changes the environment
by putting a bunch of cues

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:40.600
that you saw before
on the belt and fixes

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.790
the reward right in
the middle, here.

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.190
And you can see, the
evolution of the licking

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.330
is quite rapidly--

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:49.750
certainly, by 30, 40,
50 trials, the animal

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:53.620
has figured out where the reward
is and only licks right there.

00:09:53.620 --> 00:10:00.220
That's the behavior, and it
occurs within 10 minutes.

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:04.810
And what also changes is
that the animal begins

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:09.250
to slow down and stop,
like you saw on the video,

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:11.260
around the reward,
to consume the reward

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:12.680
and then begins running again.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:17.320
Now, we look at the same
set of animals, same fields,

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.600
microscope field, same neurons.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.580
And we can see that
there's about two,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:24.130
three times as many
place cells now

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:26.620
and that there's this
huge overrepresentation

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:27.700
at the reward side.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:30.100
Again, nearly
three-fold increase

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:33.160
in the density of place
cells that have their place

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:34.883
fields around the reward.

00:10:34.883 --> 00:10:38.410
OK, so there's an
experience-dependent shaping

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:40.240
of the representation.

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:43.270
A lot of other people study
this and have seen this before.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:47.320
This group has seen that if they
do an optogenetic manipulation

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:51.310
that removes this
overrepresentation, then

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:55.240
this learning, this evolution
of licking also doesn't happen.

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:58.995
So we would call this
an adaptive change.

00:10:58.995 --> 00:11:05.420
OK, so this is our adaptive
change in hippocampal activity.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.115
Now, what's going on?

00:11:07.115 --> 00:11:08.990
I told you there was
about two or three times

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:12.650
as many place fields
at the end of day

00:11:12.650 --> 00:11:14.850
one as from the beginning.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:17.090
And what's basically
happening is,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:21.440
there are new place fields
appearing during the session.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.900
And there are four
characteristics, four

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:27.200
or five characteristics
of these new place fields

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.660
that are relevant to what
I'm going to tell you.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.880
And so the first is that-- you
can see here, they just sort of

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:36.830
pop out.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.550
They appear abruptly.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:42.250
So no real hints that there's
any kind of activity already

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:42.750
here.

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.060
They're not sort of
ramping up over time.

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.660
They just boom, appear.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:50.000
And many people in
the hippocampal world

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.550
see place cells that do this.

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.650
Now the other thing is,
you'll see that they

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:59.180
tend to shift back in space.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:00.810
In this case, we're in space.

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.930
So the first field is here.

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:05.360
And then on the
subsequent two or three,

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:06.840
it moves back and stays there.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.420
And the same is here.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.860
It moves back and
stays there, OK?

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:18.200
And so there's this predictive
shift, step back in space.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:19.970
Next, you can
already see as well,

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.270
is that depending on the running
speed of the animal, when

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:27.380
the place field appears,
the width of the place field

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:28.790
changes as well.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.220
The faster they run,
the wider the field

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:36.130
that's going to result, OK?

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:39.700
And finally, all of
this, and the development

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.710
of the overrepresentation,
and the increase

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:44.980
in the number of place cells,
all the adaptive changes

00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:48.310
can be blocked by changes--

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:53.050
different drugs which block
plasticity in these regions,

00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:55.570
like NMDA receptor antagonist.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:59.590
And a blocker of R type
calcium channels or even

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:04.435
L type calcium channels will
remove these adaptive changes.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:10.450
OK, so let's see.

00:13:10.450 --> 00:13:15.693
So I don't really have time
to go into this too much.

00:13:15.693 --> 00:13:17.110
I'm going to show
you quickly what

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:18.650
I'm talking about in a minute.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:22.000
But synaptic plasticity, we
found over the past 10 years

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.090
or so that these place
fields are driven

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:27.130
by a synaptic plasticity, OK?

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:31.420
There's a ramp of depolarization
of the membrane potential that

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:35.980
crosses threshold and produces
then action potential firing

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:37.210
at a particular location.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:41.560
That ramp of depolarization is
produced by an enhanced weight

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.290
of the synaptic input, the
excitatory synaptic input

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:45.600
that's coming in.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:49.000
OK, so I'm going to show
you one example and then

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.210
a little bit of population data,
and then cut to the cartoon.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.130
It's unfortunate,
but if you'd like

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:58.300
to talk to me more detail
about it, I'd be happy to.

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.360
All right, so this
is the other way

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:03.470
that we record
from these animals,

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:06.037
and that is wholesale
voltage recordings

00:14:06.037 --> 00:14:07.870
while the animal's doing
the same thing that

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:09.610
have already showed you.

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:11.828
What's going to happen
is, the animal is

00:14:11.828 --> 00:14:13.120
going to be running these laps.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:14.460
And here's the VM.

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:18.190
And then the position of
the animal from 0 to 180

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:19.600
centimeters is in red.

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:21.430
And then there's an
extracellular voltage

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:25.720
that's been filtered at
theta band frequencies.

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:30.760
A dendritic calcium spike,
dendritic calcium plateau

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:33.580
potential is going to
occur on one trial.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.080
And then in the
next trial, there'll

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.120
be a place field there.

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:39.670
And these plateau
potentials occur out

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:43.420
in the distal apical dendrite.

00:14:43.420 --> 00:14:44.300
So here we go.

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:47.680
I'm going to hold it down
to make it go twice as fast.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:49.150
You can see there's one lap.

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:52.300
There's essentially
no place field there.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:53.950
And this is the animal standing.

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:55.390
Nothing's going on.

00:14:55.390 --> 00:14:58.310
There's a little bit of firing
while the animal's standing.

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:02.590
And when it starts running,
there's a flat line on the VM.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.180
We'll do one more.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.960
Pre-lap, some firing while
the animal's standing.

00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:11.400
And then, again, nothing.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:14.890
And then on the
next trial, you'll

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.050
see plateau potential go off.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.900
You'll notice nothing
out here, no spikes.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:22.660
And then on the
next trial, you'll

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:26.110
see increased depolarization
and a place field.

00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:30.790
On the next lap, these
are current injections

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:34.270
to check series resistance.

00:15:34.270 --> 00:15:37.090
And the place field is there.

00:15:37.090 --> 00:15:38.710
I think I got one more.

00:15:38.710 --> 00:15:40.600
I'll do one more lap.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:43.195
There it is, a nice place
field that's developed.

00:15:43.195 --> 00:15:45.070
And then the animal
actually forgets to stop,

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:48.880
and the place field
is even smaller.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:53.610
OK, so here's all of
the data, all the laps.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.830
You can see, the first
10 trials, nothing.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:57.750
Then plateau potential goes off.

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.922
There's no action potentials
or anything before.

00:16:00.922 --> 00:16:02.880
You know, of course,
there's something going on

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.530
with this giant calcium spike.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.690
And then afterwards,
there's a place field

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:10.260
that stays for the
additional 20 minutes

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:12.220
the animal's on the track.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:14.880
And that's what the plateau
looks like at the soma.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:18.900
It's basically a
50 or 60 millivolt,

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:23.970
500 millisecond long
calcium channel mediated

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:25.890
action potential
out on the dendrites

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:30.720
that propagate semi-actively
throughout the entire neuron,

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:35.650
broadcasting this global signal.

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:40.860
And here are these average
of these trials in gray.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.708
And now we've bent
them in space.

00:16:42.708 --> 00:16:44.500
There's a little bit
of time bends as well.

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:45.720
So you can see before.

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:49.800
And here's after the plateau.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:53.560
You can see that there's a very
large ramp of depolarization.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.340
So these are the VMs that
have been median-filtered.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:57.820
That takes the spikes off.

00:16:57.820 --> 00:16:59.970
And so you can just see
this underlying ramp

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.900
of depolarization, quite large.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:05.670
In this case, we can
subtract these two things

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:09.390
and use a time base
based on the running.

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:12.930
During this one trial, when
the plateau potential went off,

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:14.410
it's referenced to that.

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:18.390
So it takes 4 seconds to
run from the very beginning

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.130
of the track to the
plateau and then

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:24.119
another 2 or 3 seconds from
the plateau to the end.

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:25.650
And then what you
can see then is--

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:28.440
so this is just a subtraction
of these two traces--

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:31.320
that this change in
the membrane potential

00:17:31.320 --> 00:17:35.550
is covering about 6 seconds.

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:38.970
So it's a multi-second
long plasticity--

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:42.450
and that it is also asymmetric
and that it's projecting

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:45.450
further back than forwards.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:48.610
And you can see that the peak
is actually shifted as well.

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:52.170
So it produces the second
thing that I showed you.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:54.720
First of all, it's abrupt.

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.850
It's abrupt because
these plateau potentials

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:57.660
are so strong.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.910
Bam, one time and you
get a place field.

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.740
You get all the plasticity.

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:04.560
It causes the place
field to shift back

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:09.480
because of this asymmetric
plasticity kernel, OK?

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:13.845
And then it's operating
on the seconds time scale.

00:18:13.845 --> 00:18:16.560
And depending on how fast
the animal is running,

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:19.110
the width will also
vary accordingly.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:21.990
All of this can be
blocked by, again,

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:26.250
NMDA receptor antagonists, both
in vivo and in vitro, as well

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:29.650
as calcium channel blockers.

00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:30.870
Here's from the population.

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:34.350
Again, it's asymmetric,
covering multiple seconds.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:38.700
These are all cells that didn't
have really much of a ramp.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:41.640
We call them silent cells
because they're silent.

00:18:41.640 --> 00:18:44.160
But not only that, they
don't have much, even

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:48.520
a subthreshold change in
the membrane potential.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:53.370
So about 2/3 of these, we've
actually injected current into

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.170
to drive the plateau potentials.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:59.820
So if we have a cell like
this and we inject current

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.750
somewhere else, then when
a cell that has already

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:07.590
has a place field, then
the initial place field

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:08.670
will get smaller.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:10.320
Let's say we inject
a current here.

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:12.300
The initial place
field will get smaller,

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:16.990
and this part would
increase in depolarization.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:20.250
So the plasticity is
not just a potentiation.

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:21.390
It's also a depression.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.400
It's bidirectional,
and they're happening

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:25.330
both at the same time.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.500
And you can see that here.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:27.930
If there's already
a place cell--

00:19:27.930 --> 00:19:31.920
and we either add plateau
potentials or one happens

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:36.030
spontaneously-- then you can
also see, in addition to less

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:40.140
potentiation, you get this
obvious depression component

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:44.503
as well, this negative change
in the membrane potential.

00:19:44.503 --> 00:19:47.760
OK, so as I'll show
you in a second,

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:52.200
that means that the sort
of equilibrium amount

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:54.030
of plasticity that
you're going to get

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.910
is dependent upon the weights
that are already there,

00:19:56.910 --> 00:20:04.440
the current weights in the
vector of this one cell.

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.770
So this is a place cell because
there's a depolarization.

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:10.350
This depolarization is
there because the weights

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:12.100
of these inputs are higher.

00:20:12.100 --> 00:20:16.980
So now they can be
depotentiated, if you will.

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:20.160
All right, so here's
the cartoon there.

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:24.600
And we'll start off with just
sort of the basic Hebbian idea.

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:25.960
Here's the postsynaptic cell.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:29.220
Here's the presynaptic cell,
correlated input, output.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:30.780
You get plasticity.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:34.860
We call this plasticity that
I've just showed you BTSP,

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:38.040
Behavioral Time Scale
Synaptic Plasticity.

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:40.330
And it requires
additional components.

00:20:40.330 --> 00:20:43.440
One is a new compartment.

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:45.600
We need an apical dendrite.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.360
And we also need a
new voltage signal,

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:51.750
instead of just having
an output signal that's

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:54.970
driving the plasticity
changes in the weights.

00:20:54.970 --> 00:20:57.540
We're going to have a
plateau potential, which

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.570
is more or less solely
responsible for producing

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:01.650
plasticity.

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:03.900
It's not really
an output signal.

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:08.590
It's just a plasticity signal,
just a plasticity signal.

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:12.450
And then we're going to need an
additional input whose job is

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:16.260
mainly to drive the
plasticity through plateau

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:19.310
potential initiation.

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:22.140
OK, and then we're also going
to need some biochemical signals

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:23.580
to stretch this
thing out in time,

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:28.140
some filters of both the
synaptic input and the plateau

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:29.640
potential.

00:21:29.640 --> 00:21:31.140
I'll just tell you
that we've been

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:32.650
trying to figure these out.

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:36.060
It's not easy, and we really
haven't made any progress.

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:39.330
That's the bottom line on that.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:42.448
It's biochemistry, and that's
a little out of our wheelhouse.

00:21:42.448 --> 00:21:43.990
And this is what
we're talking about.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:46.470
If you get too down
in the wrong layer,

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:49.020
then things start
to go sour on you.

00:21:49.020 --> 00:21:53.760
But hopefully, somebody else
can figure these things out,

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.920
because this is
still theoretical.

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:58.590
There's very little evidence
of these biochemical signals.

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:01.500
Now, I'm told [INAUDIBLE]
lab has published something

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:04.590
recently with Fred--

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:08.220
Frank Polo that
showed that there

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:11.130
may be an involvement
of calcium release,

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:14.430
calcium-induced calcium
release that may be involved

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.670
with this instructive signal.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:22.890
Anyway, the cartoon
equation is here.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:25.380
The change in the
weights is-- basically,

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.840
here's the heart of the matter,
the overlap of these two

00:22:27.840 --> 00:22:30.070
biochemical signals, OK?

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:32.070
And so this determines
which synapses--

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.440
this is to pick which
synapses are going to change.

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:36.960
Exactly which
direction and how much

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:39.160
is dependent on the
overlap as well.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:42.820
And then how far away-- this
is the potentiation component

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:43.320
here.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:46.140
In this case, if the
potentiation-- these

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:47.850
are the weights that
are already there.

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:49.560
This is the current weight.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:52.055
If it's high, of course,
then this potentiation

00:22:52.055 --> 00:22:52.930
is going to be small.

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:55.710
If it's low, it
can be quite large.

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.410
And then there's just
the sort of balance

00:22:58.410 --> 00:23:02.760
of this by the depression
component, which, again, is

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:04.920
scaled by the current weights.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:08.130
Here's the paper,
Aaron's paper in eLife,

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:09.840
where the real equation lives.

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:11.640
And you can go and see that.

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:14.890
Yeah?

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:17.530
Is that enough?

00:23:17.530 --> 00:23:19.930
That's the plasticity.

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:24.190
And now, some details--

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:25.430
I hope you like details--

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:29.500
on this instructive pathway,
and where is it coming from,

00:23:29.500 --> 00:23:33.110
what's it doing, and what
kind of signal is it.

00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:38.080
And so we knew
from the past work

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:42.850
and just sort of bloody
obvious anatomy where to look.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:46.420
Entorhinal cortex, layer three,
neurons are pyramidal cells.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:51.520
And they project a pathway that
almost exclusively innervates

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:54.580
the distal apical
tuft of these cells,

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:58.430
where this plateau
potential is initiated.

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:02.650
So Christine is going to do
an optogenetic experiment.

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:05.980
Now, she doesn't want to
blast the whole network.

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:09.010
She's trying to make a subtle
change so that the behavior

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:09.730
doesn't change.

00:24:09.730 --> 00:24:13.030
And this is a loop, somehow.

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:15.850
It's not very direct, but
the EC and the hippocampus

00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:18.340
are tied together, so she
doesn't want to inadvertently

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:22.130
change the afferent input,
the feedforward input as well.

00:24:22.130 --> 00:24:27.790
So she's going to use a
retrograde virus to just infect

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:31.390
the EC cells that are
sending their axons

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:34.510
into this particular
region of the hippocampus.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:36.910
She's also going to
infect and express

00:24:36.910 --> 00:24:39.760
GCaMP in that same
region and record

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.280
from this population
of pyramidal cells,

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:45.250
put a light fiber
in, that you see,

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:49.750
to activate the archaerhodopsin
or at least the control

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:53.693
conditions, just cells
that are expressing tomato.

00:24:53.693 --> 00:24:54.610
There's a light fiber.

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:58.990
You turn the light on around the
reward location, run the animal

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:02.600
and do everything normal, and
there's basically no effect.

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.060
You can see the
overrepresentation

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:05.020
in the middle.

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:09.580
And then in the test group
then with the archaerhodopsin,

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:12.880
she has more or less
inhibited the activity

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:16.780
of a small subset of
EC cells and changed,

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:20.800
removed the adaptive
change, the plasticity

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:24.280
in the CA1 hippocampal
region, OK?

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:28.750
Manipulate an external region
and change the plasticity

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:30.790
in this one region.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:32.290
Really nice.

00:25:32.290 --> 00:25:36.646
And you can see, that's
quantified there.

00:25:36.646 --> 00:25:39.850
OK, so something's
going on in the EC.

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:43.750
It's somehow sending
some input that's

00:25:43.750 --> 00:25:50.140
helping to drive or direct
this plasticity in CA1.

00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:52.390
Next step is to record
from these axons

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:54.730
and see what kind of
activity they have.

00:25:54.730 --> 00:25:57.580
Bottom line is that
there's about 5%

00:25:57.580 --> 00:25:59.860
and maybe less
than 10% that shows

00:25:59.860 --> 00:26:02.020
some decent spatial selectivity.

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:04.750
But most of them
are these kind of

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:09.970
crappy, sloppy,
place-specific cells

00:26:09.970 --> 00:26:14.050
that have some specific firing
at a particular location

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:15.620
in the world.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:19.780
You can see, here's
one cell, many trials.

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:21.460
Position that I
didn't label here.

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:22.580
It is position.

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:26.350
And you can see that there's a
stochasticity to this firing.

00:26:26.350 --> 00:26:28.180
It's a little bit weird, OK?

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:30.220
And so that led
us to try to look

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:34.870
at the distribution of these
on times and off times.

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:38.680
And it's true that you need
hundreds of these openings,

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:40.960
and there's only 20
cells out of 1,000

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:43.870
that we had this
many transitions.

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:49.270
But in those cells, there is
an exponential distribution

00:26:49.270 --> 00:26:54.500
of the on and off
times of these cells.

00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.970
So bottom line is, there's
some moderate tuning,

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:01.330
moderate spatial
tuning of the EC input,

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:04.360
with hints of some kind
of nonstandard activity.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:08.600
OK, so here's another hint
of nonstandard activity.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.830
Here's all of the
800 cells for this.

00:27:11.830 --> 00:27:14.200
We're still in the
same environment

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:16.120
that I showed you before.

00:27:16.120 --> 00:27:23.440
And here's the average of the
odd lab trials, 0, 2, 4, 6, 8,

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:24.290
et cetera.

00:27:24.290 --> 00:27:26.500
And you can see they've
been sorted according

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:28.330
to the location of their peak.

00:27:28.330 --> 00:27:31.720
And then if we look at the
same cells sorted the same way

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:37.345
but for the intervening
laps, 1, 3, 5, 9,

00:27:37.345 --> 00:27:41.560
you can see that there's
some stochasticity there.

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:43.240
They're not very stable.

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:47.300
But there is a little
hint of stability here.

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:49.840
And then if we plot
these locations

00:27:49.840 --> 00:27:52.480
for the even-- the peak
locations for the even laps

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:55.810
versus the odd laps, it's
not exactly a shotgun blast,

00:27:55.810 --> 00:27:57.160
but pretty close.

00:27:57.160 --> 00:28:02.110
There is only 20% of these
cells that have some stability

00:28:02.110 --> 00:28:04.330
to their finding.

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:06.160
We made a model of
all this activity

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:09.340
in order to try
to understand how

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:12.010
it would be summed
up postsynaptically

00:28:12.010 --> 00:28:14.890
and drive plateau potentials.

00:28:14.890 --> 00:28:17.110
Unfortunately, I don't have
time to talk about that.

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:22.060
But I'd be happy to answer
questions about it at any time.

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:26.500
The basic idea is that
we just reproduce--

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:28.930
it's a very simple
model, but it really

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:32.710
captures a lot of the
activity in an amazing way.

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:36.130
2,000 two-state Markov
chains, with a little bit

00:28:36.130 --> 00:28:39.760
of tweaking on the transition
probabilities, the activation

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:42.430
probability, to produce
the sloppy tuning.

00:28:42.430 --> 00:28:45.850
And then we just
distribute them equally,

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:47.770
uniformly across space.

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:49.540
And that matches the data.

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:52.210
This is basically a
quantification of that.

00:28:52.210 --> 00:28:53.930
And the model does
the same thing.

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:58.120
Now, in the model, we
can simulate the mouse

00:28:58.120 --> 00:28:59.650
running at a constant rate.

00:28:59.650 --> 00:29:02.290
And lo and behold,
there is a constant rate

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:05.410
of plateaus that are
initiated across the track.

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:06.730
Not surprising.

00:29:06.730 --> 00:29:10.570
What is interesting about
this is how low this rate is.

00:29:10.570 --> 00:29:15.910
So it's 0.5 times 10 to the
minus 3 per second per cell.

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:17.920
So there's 10,000
postsynaptic cells

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:21.110
in this case, 50
laps, 10 seconds.

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:24.370
That gives you about
2,500 plateau potentials,

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:28.150
2,500 new place
cells out of 10,000.

00:29:28.150 --> 00:29:30.280
So even at this
incredibly low rate,

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:33.430
you can significantly change
the number of place cells

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:36.100
that are in the population.

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:40.120
Just for comparison, if you
had 40%, 4,000 of your cells

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:44.050
were place cells, and they had
10 hertz firing for 1 second,

00:29:44.050 --> 00:29:47.080
you'd get 1,000 times
more action potentials

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:48.980
than you would
plateau potentials.

00:29:48.980 --> 00:29:51.940
So it is a very low
rate of occurrence

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:55.030
of plateau potentials,
which fits with the data.

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:59.110
Now, if the animal's obviously
not running at a constant speed

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:02.440
but is slowing down
around the reward site,

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:07.780
here's the real data for that
average of 14 said animals.

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:10.250
And if we use that instead
of this constant rate,

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:13.150
then you can see that the
constant rate of plateau

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:15.040
potentials where an
animal is actually

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:18.400
dwelling for twice as
long around the reward

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:20.980
will give you twice as
many plateau potentials,

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:23.530
twice as many place
cells at that location.

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.490
OK, it's that simple.

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:29.410
And so that's our prediction
of what all of this activity

00:30:29.410 --> 00:30:33.940
should give you in terms
of a representation in CA1.

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:36.580
Here's the actual
representation in CA1 in black,

00:30:36.580 --> 00:30:38.120
and now this is in gray.

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:41.800
You can see that, yeah, it's in
the right place, it's two-fold,

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:45.550
but the data is actually
a three-fold increase.

00:30:45.550 --> 00:30:49.360
And there is a significant
correlation here.

00:30:49.360 --> 00:30:52.840
Now, you can say, oh, well,
this was a very simple model.

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:54.320
You're lucky to
have gotten that.

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:56.600
And that's pretty
much what we said.

00:30:56.600 --> 00:30:57.985
But there is a difference.

00:30:57.985 --> 00:30:59.860
And that's what I was
going to show you here.

00:30:59.860 --> 00:31:02.350
There's actually a
little more tuning

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:04.870
of-- the cells around the
reward site are actually

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:06.700
a little bit more tuned.

00:31:06.700 --> 00:31:09.850
And maybe that's
what's going on there

00:31:09.850 --> 00:31:12.640
or maybe we should just
count our lucky stars

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.630
and move on, which
is what we did.

00:31:14.630 --> 00:31:18.940
So bottom line is, there's
a uniform distribution

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:21.610
of these moderately tuned cells.

00:31:21.610 --> 00:31:26.140
So that, plus the running
behavior of the animal,

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:30.700
basically explains
the change or the type

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:33.880
of distribution of
place cells that we

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:35.890
see in the CA1 representation.

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:39.010
Now, it also
suggested to us that--

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:40.930
you can remember, the
somatosensory cues

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:44.140
were uniformly distributed
across the track.

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:48.850
So we thought, maybe that
uniform distribution of the EC

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:53.890
activity is representing
that uniform distribution

00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:56.337
of the cues in the environment.

00:31:56.337 --> 00:31:58.420
And so we're going to
switch and try to test that.

00:31:58.420 --> 00:32:02.380
We're going to remove all the
cues, and have a blank belt,

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:07.810
and just use a light
stimulus 50 centimeters

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:08.930
in front of the reward.

00:32:08.930 --> 00:32:11.162
So it's predictive, and
it's strong, it's novel.

00:32:11.162 --> 00:32:12.370
The animal's never seen this.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:15.190
It's a flashing light right
in its eyes, 10 hertz.

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:17.230
You can see that the
licking behavior is

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:19.390
subtly changed, but
not too dramatically,

00:32:19.390 --> 00:32:22.070
but that the running
behavior is quite different.

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:24.760
So here's what I just showed
you in black for environment A.

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:27.580
And now, in environment
B, the animal

00:32:27.580 --> 00:32:29.860
starts to slow down
around the light stimulus,

00:32:29.860 --> 00:32:32.920
and then just continues to
gradually slow, and then

00:32:32.920 --> 00:32:35.930
continue on after the reward.

00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:38.320
So what do we get from the EC?

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:39.370
Now 800 cells.

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:41.230
Again, the same idea.

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:44.320
Here is the even trial,
the odd trials, and then

00:32:44.320 --> 00:32:47.020
the average of the even trials.

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:52.870
You can again see that there's a
lot of stochasticity still left

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:53.530
in this.

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:55.540
But you can see the shadow here.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:59.030
Now there's about three or five
times as many of these cells.

00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:01.150
And again, on this
plot here, right

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:08.370
at the site of the light that
we didn't see before, here

00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:11.040
this is quantified again.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:12.840
There's about a
four-fold increase

00:33:12.840 --> 00:33:17.910
in the density of the EC
cells whose peak activity

00:33:17.910 --> 00:33:19.240
is around the light.

00:33:19.240 --> 00:33:20.940
There's the model in
the lighter colors,

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:24.030
which are difficult to see.

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:25.610
So that's the model.

00:33:25.610 --> 00:33:28.250
And then we do the same
thing with the actual running

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:29.550
versus the constant.

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:31.970
Here's the prediction
of the plateaus

00:33:31.970 --> 00:33:33.800
in the postsynaptic population.

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:36.140
You can see that
there is a difference.

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:38.750
Again, the running is
making a difference.

00:33:38.750 --> 00:33:41.420
But because it's
not just as distinct

00:33:41.420 --> 00:33:45.080
as it was before in
the environment A,

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:46.730
there's not as big
of a difference.

00:33:46.730 --> 00:33:50.960
And really, there's a
big change in the density

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:54.050
of plateaus around the light.

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:57.560
That kind of tapers
off slowly as you go.

00:33:57.560 --> 00:34:00.980
Now, so that's the prediction of
what the CA1 should look like,

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:05.000
the distribution of
place cells in the CA1

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:06.320
in this new environment.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:07.080
Here it is.

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:09.020
You can see the
overrepresentation there.

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:10.940
Here it is quantified.

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:15.960
There's about a two, three-fold
increase in the CA1 place

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:17.449
cells around the light.

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:21.260
Less of an effect, maybe
75% increase verses

00:34:21.260 --> 00:34:24.350
two to three-fold increase in B.

00:34:24.350 --> 00:34:29.659
Here are the prediction,
in light, colored red,

00:34:29.659 --> 00:34:32.540
from the model and then
the actual from the CA1

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:35.300
data, in the darker color.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:38.070
Again, there's a significant
correlation between those two.

00:34:38.070 --> 00:34:41.570
So it looks like
then that the EC

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:47.120
is adjusting itself, its
activity to the density

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:50.989
or the distribution of relevant
cues in the environment

00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:53.360
and using that as
a training signal

00:34:53.360 --> 00:34:57.350
or teaching signal to tell the--

00:34:57.350 --> 00:34:59.823
to shape the type
of representation

00:34:59.823 --> 00:35:00.740
that's present in CA1.

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:08.230
All right, last slide.

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:11.350
What kind of instructive
signal is this?

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:13.210
Is it an error
signal like we would

00:35:13.210 --> 00:35:17.240
expect like in the Purkinje
cells in the cerebellum?

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:19.690
So here's something that
I didn't show you before.

00:35:19.690 --> 00:35:23.500
Here's the number or the
fraction of place cells,

00:35:23.500 --> 00:35:26.800
new place cells, that are
appearing during the trial, OK?

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:30.220
So you can see, in these
sections of 25 laps,

00:35:30.220 --> 00:35:33.460
that about 3/4 of the
cells that are going

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:37.360
to appear within
the first 25 trials.

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:41.020
And then this drops like a
rock, such that by 50 trials,

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:44.140
there's essentially very
little plasticity left.

00:35:47.122 --> 00:35:52.010
OK, so if this were
an error signal,

00:35:52.010 --> 00:35:56.460
we would expect it to track this
same sort of plasticity profile

00:35:56.460 --> 00:35:56.960
here.

00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:01.280
Instead, our measure of EC
activity in both environments

00:36:01.280 --> 00:36:03.690
stays relatively constant.

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:08.030
So this suggests to us that this
is not an error signal coming

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:11.780
in from the internal cortex
and that it's probably

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:16.040
more like a desired or target
signal that's coming in.

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:18.530
And so we can make
the control system

00:36:18.530 --> 00:36:23.570
analogy, the weak analogy,
again, where we have--

00:36:23.570 --> 00:36:26.240
here's the components
of this here.

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:30.600
Again, the basis
set comes up from--

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:33.020
so there's a feedforward
input from a CA3

00:36:33.020 --> 00:36:34.940
of already tuned place cells.

00:36:34.940 --> 00:36:38.270
And then there's a target coming
in from the entorhinal cortex.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:41.990
There's population
activity in CA1

00:36:41.990 --> 00:36:46.610
that's fed back through
somatostatin positive O-LM

00:36:46.610 --> 00:36:50.870
interneurons who, again,
exclusively innervate

00:36:50.870 --> 00:36:53.810
the tuft of these
pyramidal cells in CA1.

00:36:53.810 --> 00:36:58.290
This is obviously
an inhibitory input.

00:36:58.290 --> 00:37:03.170
And so the apical dendrite
tuft of these cells,

00:37:03.170 --> 00:37:05.570
this distal region
of the tree, is

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:09.170
acting like a comparator
for these two activities

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:10.970
in two different
brain regions, OK?

00:37:10.970 --> 00:37:13.340
The activity in the
entorhinal cortex

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:18.770
is compared with the activity
in the CA1 via this feedback

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:19.700
inhibition.

00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:23.660
And if the target
exceeds the inhibition,

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:26.540
the actual, then
there's an error local

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:28.730
in each individual
pyramidal cell,

00:37:28.730 --> 00:37:31.400
and that error is the
plateau potential.

00:37:31.400 --> 00:37:34.820
The plateau potential
then produces plasticity.

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:38.686
That changes the
actual CA1 activity.

00:37:38.686 --> 00:37:43.130
OK, and so if we
look at this in time,

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:45.290
we remove the real data
and put the cartoon up--

00:37:45.290 --> 00:37:46.710
that's always nice--

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:50.540
we can see, the activity
from the target, from the EC

00:37:50.540 --> 00:37:54.410
is up here, where the
activity in CA1 is down here.

00:37:54.410 --> 00:37:56.490
There's a difference
in the activity.

00:37:56.490 --> 00:37:58.940
This produces a large
amount of plasticity

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:01.670
in CA1 in plateau
potentials that

00:38:01.670 --> 00:38:07.880
increases the activity in or
just brings it closer in space

00:38:07.880 --> 00:38:09.740
to the target.

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:13.960
That reduces the plasticity
and turns everything off.

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:16.540
So as these two activities
approach each other,

00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:21.160
this reduces the error signal
in the individual local regions

00:38:21.160 --> 00:38:26.440
of CA1 pyramidal cells and
cuts the plasticity off.

00:38:26.440 --> 00:38:28.390
All right, so that's it.

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:29.950
There it is in space, same idea.

00:38:33.187 --> 00:38:34.020
I'll spare you this.

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:37.410
Basically, it's that it appears
that the entorhinal cortex

00:38:37.410 --> 00:38:40.500
in this case is
collecting external input

00:38:40.500 --> 00:38:43.980
and internal input from self
motion and maybe decision

00:38:43.980 --> 00:38:46.050
making areas or
something and producing

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:49.950
a kind of quick and dirty
estimation of what's

00:38:49.950 --> 00:38:51.100
important in the world.

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:54.210
And that's using--
and then sending out

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:57.690
targets based upon this sort of
quick and dirty understanding

00:38:57.690 --> 00:39:00.700
about the aspects of the world
to train the hippocampus,

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:03.210
which in the end
shapes itself depending

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:06.210
on what the hippocampus and
what the EC thinks is important.

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:08.700
And because all
of this plasticity

00:39:08.700 --> 00:39:11.250
produces shifts in
the place fields,

00:39:11.250 --> 00:39:12.480
these are now predictions.

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:15.360
And so one of the ideas is,
actually, these predictions

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:17.700
may then be targets
themselves that

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:21.750
get sent, broadcast
throughout the entire brain

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:26.640
to help it learn as well.

00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:29.100
All right, so let me
mention Christine.

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:31.210
She did almost everything
that I showed you today.

00:39:31.210 --> 00:39:34.710
And she's your neighbor
now up at Brandeis.

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:36.390
She's very talented.

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:37.950
Look her up some time.

00:39:37.950 --> 00:39:43.920
And then Sachin did
some of GCaMP imaging.

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:46.020
Yiding and another student
in the lab, of course,

00:39:46.020 --> 00:39:49.560
with Katie, did all of
the wholesale recordings.

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:51.000
And Randy keeps things running.

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:53.190
We still have a lot
of good interactions

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:54.780
with our old
colleagues at Janelia.

00:39:54.780 --> 00:39:55.981
Thanks.

00:39:55.981 --> 00:39:59.348
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:03.628 --> 00:40:04.670
MARK: Questions for Jeff?

00:40:13.690 --> 00:40:14.860
AUDIENCE: Thank you, Jeff.

00:40:14.860 --> 00:40:16.010
That was great.

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:19.690
I'm wondering about, given that
the synaptic weight affects

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:23.140
the expression of
BTSP, when you expose

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:26.140
the animal to a second
new environment,

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:29.470
is there a relationship
in the same cells

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:32.863
of their new
preferred place field?

00:40:32.863 --> 00:40:34.780
JEFF MAGEE: Is there a
relationship with what?

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:38.500
AUDIENCE: In the-- if you
have new place fields forming

00:40:38.500 --> 00:40:41.350
in that second environment,
do you see a correlation

00:40:41.350 --> 00:40:45.190
in the previous place
field location--

00:40:45.190 --> 00:40:46.600
JEFF MAGEE: A prior memory.

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:48.980
Yes, we're looking at that.

00:40:48.980 --> 00:40:50.260
That's all I can say.

00:40:50.260 --> 00:40:54.600
That's what Sachin's working on.

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:55.560
MARK: Very savvy, Jeff.

00:40:55.560 --> 00:40:57.005
Nice save.

00:40:57.005 --> 00:40:58.380
JEFF MAGEE: I
can't say anything.

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:00.002
He'd kill me.

00:41:00.002 --> 00:41:00.502
Yes?

00:41:04.597 --> 00:41:05.930
AUDIENCE: Yeah, thank you, Jeff.

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:08.870
That was really interesting.

00:41:08.870 --> 00:41:13.940
This rapid dropoff in
place field formation

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:15.110
that happens like--

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:17.810
you get a lot and then
suddenly there's very little.

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:21.620
Is that because the entorhinal
cortex is sending out

00:41:21.620 --> 00:41:23.870
fewer blasts?

00:41:23.870 --> 00:41:27.290
Or is that because the blasts
are not being registered or not

00:41:27.290 --> 00:41:29.540
doing anything?

00:41:29.540 --> 00:41:33.620
JEFF MAGEE: Well, so
probably, just the way

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:36.290
that I expressed this
made it look a little more

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:37.340
jumpy than it is.

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:39.840
This is in blocks, OK?

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.650
So it's 25 then 25 trials.

00:41:42.650 --> 00:41:46.610
But the point is that the EC
activity really isn't changing.

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:48.110
I just showed you
a very simple sort

00:41:48.110 --> 00:41:51.590
of overall sum of the average,
but the population vector

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:55.310
isn't changing much
either as the animal goes

00:41:55.310 --> 00:41:56.880
through the entire session.

00:41:56.880 --> 00:42:01.220
So it has to be
something else that's

00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:03.050
turning the plasticity off.

00:42:03.050 --> 00:42:07.700
And that's where the control
system thing comes in, where

00:42:07.700 --> 00:42:12.260
the feedback from the O-LM
interneurons which innervate

00:42:12.260 --> 00:42:13.430
the plateau--

00:42:13.430 --> 00:42:16.730
these are somastatin positive
cells, like Martinotti cells--

00:42:16.730 --> 00:42:20.360
actually shuts down all the
plasticity, the plateaus.

00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:23.290
Nothing changes in the EC.

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:27.810
It still thinks, OK, this
is what you should be doing.

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:29.360
AUDIENCE: But if
you took the mouse

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:31.110
and you put it in a
different environment,

00:42:31.110 --> 00:42:35.100
like after 25 trials,
then it would still

00:42:35.100 --> 00:42:36.660
be able to learn
the new environment.

00:42:36.660 --> 00:42:38.342
I mean, it could
learn constantly.

00:42:38.342 --> 00:42:40.800
It just doesn't like to learn
more in the same environment.

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:41.355
Is that--

00:42:41.355 --> 00:42:42.480
JEFF MAGEE: That's correct.

00:42:42.480 --> 00:42:46.500
And the idea would be just
that the activity of CA1

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:48.730
is approaching the target, OK?

00:42:48.730 --> 00:42:50.910
And so if you put it
in a new environment,

00:42:50.910 --> 00:42:52.920
then the target would
change, and that

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:54.820
would drive new plasticity.

00:42:54.820 --> 00:42:57.120
And so this is
oversimplified, OK?

00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:00.300
There's obviously other
things, attentional modulation

00:43:00.300 --> 00:43:03.710
and other things like that,
that are going to be involved.

00:43:03.710 --> 00:43:05.727
And this is the most
theoretical part of it.

00:43:05.727 --> 00:43:07.560
We don't really know
what's shutting it off,

00:43:07.560 --> 00:43:08.850
but this is our theory.

00:43:08.850 --> 00:43:12.610
And Aaron's made a model of
this in his paper as well,

00:43:12.610 --> 00:43:13.750
and it works.

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:17.340
And so that's the
idea, Bartlett,

00:43:17.340 --> 00:43:19.710
that there's some component
within the network that's

00:43:19.710 --> 00:43:23.100
shutting the plasticity off as
the activities come together.

00:43:25.690 --> 00:43:27.370
Yeah.

00:43:27.370 --> 00:43:30.220
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:33.220
So you discovered a new
type of plasticity, right?

00:43:33.220 --> 00:43:35.680
The BTSP, in vivo.

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:40.670
People have been trying
to look at plasticity

00:43:40.670 --> 00:43:44.690
in vitro and slices
for many, many years.

00:43:44.690 --> 00:43:47.190
So what is the
implication of this?

00:43:47.190 --> 00:43:52.370
Do you expect that we will find
many more different plasticity

00:43:52.370 --> 00:43:56.060
rules that we couldn't
find before in vitro?

00:43:56.060 --> 00:43:59.180
Or can you tell us a little
bit why you're able to do this?

00:43:59.180 --> 00:44:00.680
JEFF MAGEE: I think
there are enough

00:44:00.680 --> 00:44:03.380
plasticity in vitro rules.

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:06.650
Hopefully we won't
find too many more.

00:44:06.650 --> 00:44:08.400
This one is in vivo.

00:44:08.400 --> 00:44:09.980
This one shows up in vivo.

00:44:09.980 --> 00:44:15.080
We haven't done the LTD, the
depotentiation portion of it,

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:16.903
but we need to.

00:44:16.903 --> 00:44:18.320
Hopefully, other
people will start

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:21.073
looking at other brain
areas, the neocortex.

00:44:21.073 --> 00:44:22.490
It'd be interesting
to see if this

00:44:22.490 --> 00:44:25.730
is present in other
pyramidal based circuits.

00:44:25.730 --> 00:44:28.890
Like Bartlett showed you,
this is a canonical theme.

00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:31.080
It's the same circuit
here as it is everywhere.

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:32.690
So this could happen.

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:34.650
Like, your 5-cells are
a little bit different.

00:44:34.650 --> 00:44:37.520
They're like twice as long and
there's more complicated stuff

00:44:37.520 --> 00:44:38.090
going on.

00:44:38.090 --> 00:44:39.832
So it would be really
interesting to see

00:44:39.832 --> 00:44:40.790
if it's going on there.

00:44:40.790 --> 00:44:43.130
Now, we'll tell
you, we've looked

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:46.550
at least in vivo in the
previous hippocampal stage,

00:44:46.550 --> 00:44:47.960
and this is also there.

00:44:47.960 --> 00:44:51.920
There's a plateau-based,
seconds-long plasticity

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:57.020
that's responsible for
place fields in CA3.

00:44:57.020 --> 00:45:00.620
We've toyed around with other
pyramidal-- subiculum, things

00:45:00.620 --> 00:45:04.250
like this, and it seems
to be there as well.

00:45:04.250 --> 00:45:07.120
So this is--

00:45:07.120 --> 00:45:08.950
I think, at least
in the hippocampus,

00:45:08.950 --> 00:45:12.250
it's established now, and
multiple labs have seen it.

00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:14.830
The question, I think,
is how widespread it is.

00:45:14.830 --> 00:45:16.720
It going to be just hippocampus?

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:18.610
Is it going to be neocortex too?

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:19.960
Just pyramidal cells?

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:21.550
How about the granule cells?

00:45:21.550 --> 00:45:22.970
How about stellate cells?

00:45:22.970 --> 00:45:24.548
Things like that.

00:45:24.548 --> 00:45:26.590
You know, I would encourage
everybody to do this.

00:45:26.590 --> 00:45:28.600
It's not the easiest
thing in the world

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:31.510
to get a calcium
spike in a slice.

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:33.220
It takes modulation,
and then you've

00:45:33.220 --> 00:45:35.500
got to deal with all
this other changes,

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:39.040
constantly changing conditions,
if you dump muscarine

00:45:39.040 --> 00:45:40.820
on or something like that.

00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:43.930
And so we may have to
refine the conditions.

00:45:43.930 --> 00:45:47.185
But I would still-- whoever
is interested in this--

00:45:47.185 --> 00:45:50.140
this is a very different
form of plasticity

00:45:50.140 --> 00:45:53.140
and learning than standard
Hebbian kinds of things

00:45:53.140 --> 00:45:57.190
that all of us, really, myself
included, studied for 50 years.

00:45:57.190 --> 00:46:00.470
So it's worth looking into.

00:46:00.470 --> 00:46:02.970
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:06.230
MARK: One more question.

00:46:06.230 --> 00:46:07.190
You.

00:46:07.190 --> 00:46:09.490
AUDIENCE: I thought
it was the onset

00:46:09.490 --> 00:46:13.598
of this phenomenon [INAUDIBLE]

00:46:13.598 --> 00:46:16.005
JEFF MAGEE: That's cool, yeah.

00:46:16.005 --> 00:46:17.950
AUDIENCE: And even for
an engineered signal,

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:24.310
usually it starts gradually,
maybe like just [INAUDIBLE] So

00:46:24.310 --> 00:46:27.010
the fact that it's so discrete--

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:29.650
any kind of ideas
or speculation?

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:31.660
JEFF MAGEE: Well, so
it's only discrete in

00:46:31.660 --> 00:46:33.850
the individual components, OK?

00:46:33.850 --> 00:46:39.340
I mean, so it still occurs over
50 trials, tens of minutes.

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:43.630
And it is only discrete
and individual cells,

00:46:43.630 --> 00:46:46.600
which in the past, with the
Hebbian kinds of plasticities,

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:48.790
STDP and all of these
things, it would

00:46:48.790 --> 00:46:52.150
take dozens of these trials
in the individual cells.

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:56.890
So not only would the overall
population be slowly evolving,

00:46:56.890 --> 00:46:59.350
but the individual neurons
within the population

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:02.030
would be slowly expressing
this plasticity as well,

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:05.080
which may be very
important behavior--

00:47:05.080 --> 00:47:09.380
developmentally, to develop
these different receptive

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:09.880
fields.

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:12.790
Probably, it is doing something
Hebbian over many weeks

00:47:12.790 --> 00:47:14.240
or something like that.

00:47:14.240 --> 00:47:17.510
But in this case, it
is abrupt and sudden

00:47:17.510 --> 00:47:20.450
within the individual neurons.

00:47:20.450 --> 00:47:22.070
And that allows the
whole population

00:47:22.070 --> 00:47:25.190
to evolve more rapidly, but
not over the individual.

00:47:25.190 --> 00:47:28.100
We're only talking
a few tens of cells

00:47:28.100 --> 00:47:31.160
within the whole population
per trial, something like that.

00:47:33.788 --> 00:47:35.330
AUDIENCE: We know
this, but then it's

00:47:35.330 --> 00:47:39.345
really hard to find the
growth of the population.

00:47:39.345 --> 00:47:41.720
JEFF MAGEE: Well, so this is
the level of the population.

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:42.500
It's not as sharp.

00:47:42.500 --> 00:47:43.625
It just looks like it here.

00:47:43.625 --> 00:47:45.380
This is 50 trials in.

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:48.330
Two points, but we've
done it this way.

00:47:48.330 --> 00:47:52.920
And what does correlate with
this is that licking behavior.

00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:55.790
So if you plotted these
two versus the evolution

00:47:55.790 --> 00:47:59.660
of the licking, the number of
the fraction of licks outside

00:47:59.660 --> 00:48:04.830
of the reward site, they
map almost perfectly.

00:48:04.830 --> 00:48:06.230
Now, we don't like to--

00:48:06.230 --> 00:48:09.200
I don't really like to talk
about that in the paper

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:11.370
or anything like that
because then it's obvious,

00:48:11.370 --> 00:48:12.620
you should do that experiment.

00:48:12.620 --> 00:48:15.102
And we don't really
want to try to block--

00:48:15.102 --> 00:48:17.060
other people have already
done that experiment.

00:48:17.060 --> 00:48:18.510
It's hard to block.

00:48:18.510 --> 00:48:20.780
Hippocampus is this
huge sprawling thing,

00:48:20.780 --> 00:48:25.350
and to actually manipulate the
whole thing is not that easy.

00:48:25.350 --> 00:48:26.688
And so we haven't done that.

00:48:26.688 --> 00:48:27.605
But other people have.

00:48:30.880 --> 00:48:32.095
OK, thanks a lot.

00:48:32.095 --> 00:48:33.220
MARK: Thanks to Jeff again.

00:48:33.220 --> 00:48:35.070
[APPLAUSE]

